<TopicPage slug="subjects/forestry/foundational/forests-and-forestry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Forestry is the management of long-lived ecosystems for multiple, competing values: wood,
    water, wildlife, carbon and people. Unlike most land uses, the crop takes decades to grow — so
    a forester's decisions today describe a forest someone else will harvest. That time horizon,
    plus the fact that forests are habitats as much as resources, is what makes the discipline
    distinctive.
  </p>
  <p>
    The time horizon is worth dwelling on because it drives everything else in the profession. A
    wheat farmer can change crops next season; a forester who clears a stand of 80-year oaks
    cannot buy 80-year oaks for the next 80 years. Every decision is therefore a loan against
    the future, and the discipline's founding question — <em>how much can be taken, how often,
    without diminishing the source?</em> — is really an intergenerational contract. The second
    distinctive feature follows from the first: because the crop is simultaneously an ecosystem,
    every harvest is also a habitat decision, a water decision and a carbon decision. Forestry
    is where ecology and economics are forced to share a spreadsheet, and this chapter walks
    both sides of it.
  </p>

  <h2>What forests are</h2>
  <p>
    Formally, a forest is land dominated by trees — the FAO's working definition asks for
    tree canopy over 10% of the ground and an area above half a hectare — but the formal
    definition undersells the variety. <strong>Boreal</strong> forests ring the subarctic in
    a belt of pine, spruce, fir and birch across Scandinavia, Russia, Alaska and Canada:
    slow-growing, fire-shaped, over huge continuous areas. <strong>Temperate</strong> forests
    (the deciduous and mixed woods of Europe, eastern North America, East Asia; the conifer
    rainforests of the Pacific Northwest) grow on the richest soils and carry the wood
    industries' historic centres. <strong>Tropical</strong> forests — the humid rainforests
    of Amazonia, the Congo basin and Southeast Asia, plus the seasonally dry tropical
    woodlands — hold most of the world's forest biodiversity and carbon, grow with
    competition so fierce that a seedling may wait decades for a light gap. Structure
    matters more than species lists: a forest is layered (canopy, understorey, floor, soil)
    and those layers are distinct habitats — many birds and beetles never leave one stratum.
    Two functional facts define what a forest <em>does</em>: it is the largest living
    carbon store on land — trees and forest soils together hold hundreds of billions of
    tonnes of carbon, a pool on the same order of magnitude as the atmosphere's own — and it is a water machine — canopies intercept rain, roots open the soil,
    and a forested catchment yields steadier, cleaner flow than any other land cover. So the
    answer to "what is a forest?" is three answers: a biological community, a carbon
    warehouse and a hydrological instrument.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a plantation of even-aged pines is "not a forest". By
  definition it is — and the argument it provokes is the discipline's central one in
  miniature: it stores carbon and produces fibre efficiently, but supports far less
  biodiversity and (with dark, dense, single-species canopy) often less water regulation
  than a natural stand. Whether a plantation is good forestry depends entirely on what it
  replaced and what it is managed for. The honest unit of judgement is the value ledger,
  below, not the tree count.</p>

  <h2>A short history of forestry: from alarm to science</h2>
  <p>
    Modern forestry began in 18th-century Germany with a simple accounting idea: harvest no more
    wood per year than the forest grows. Sustained yield made forestry a science of rotations —
    plant, tend, harvest, regenerate — and later broadened into sustained yield of <em>everything</em>
    a forest provides. Its modern descendants are certification schemes and multi-criteria
    management plans.
  </p>
  <p>
    The idea arrived as an emergency response. By the early 1700s, German mining districts faced
    a genuine <em>Holznot</em> — timber shortage — because shafts, smelters and salt-boiling
    consumed forests faster than unmanaged woodland regenerated. Hans Carl von Carlowitz's
    <em>Sylvicultura oeconomica</em> (1713) answered with the principle of
    <strong>nachhaltende Nutzung</strong> — sustainable use — the first written statement that
    harvest must respect regrowth; Georg Ludwig Hartig and the forest academies turned it into
    mathematics (the normal forest: age classes in equal areas, yielding an even annual cut),
    and the German model was exported worldwide — to British India (forest departments from the
    1860s), the United States (the Forest Service, 1905, under Gifford Pinchot, trained at
    Nancy and shaped by German practice), and beyond. The export carried a politics: European
    state forestry often criminalised long-established local uses (common grazing, gathering,
    slash-and-burn), and the resulting conflicts shaped forest policy debates that persist in
    every former colony. The twentieth century broadened the goal from sustained yield of
    <em>timber</em> to <em>multiple use</em> (US Multiple-Use Sustained-Yield Act, 1960) and
    then to <em>ecosystem management</em>; the twenty-first added certification (FSC, 1993;
    PEFC) and carbon accounting. History's arc: alarm → accounting → science → negotiation —
    and the negotiation stage is where forestry now lives.
  </p>

  <h2>Forest values and stakeholders: the ledger</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="what forests provide — the value ledger" widget="equationStep" config={valuesCfg} />
  <p>
    The five ledger rows compete, and the competition is the profession's daily work. Wood and
    fibre remain the largest material harvest — roughly <strong>2 billion m³ of industrial
    roundwood a year</strong>, plus even more fuelwood globally — and wood's green credentials
    are real but conditional: as a material it stores carbon and substitutes for steel and
    concrete, but only regrowth (or clearly growing stocks) earns that credit. Water regulation
    is why so many great cities (New York's Catskill watersheds is the textbook case) drink
    from managed or protected forests — filtration for free, provided the catchment stays
    wooded. Biodiversity habitat makes every forestry decision a conservation decision:
    forests hold the majority of terrestrial species, and the difference between a clearcut,
    a shelterwood and a selection system is, to a beetle or a warbler, the difference between
    catastrophe, compromise and continuity. Climate services cut both ways — standing forest
    stores carbon while deforestation (roughly 10–12% of anthropogenic emissions in recent
    accounting years, with land-use change overall) transfers it back — and livelihoods and
    culture are the row economists struggle to price: hundreds of millions of people, many
    of them Indigenous or rural poor, live in or from forests.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>stakeholders</strong> are the ledger's claimants, and mapping them is the first
    step of any real management plan: timber companies and forest workers (income, jobs);
    local and Indigenous communities (foods, medicines, grazing, sacred sites — and, under
    instruments like the ILO Convention 169 and FSC's rules, consent rights); national and
    local governments (tax, revenue, watershed protection, carbon commitments); recreation
    and tourism users; conservation NGOs and future generations (whose claims are held by the
    others); and the global public, newly a stakeholder through climate policy (REDD+ —
    paying tropical countries to keep forests standing — is the global public literally
    entering the ledger). The stakeholder map explains forestry's chronic controversies
    (old-growth logging, bioenergy, plantations on community land): each is a dispute about
    which ledger row weighs most — and the discipline's mature position is that the rows are
    not independently satisfiable, so the choice must be explicit, documented and revisited.
  </p>

  <h2>The global picture: stabilising but uneven</h2>
  <p>
    The world's forest area has stopped shrinking overall — FAO's assessments show the decline
    slowing from over 10 million hectares lost per year in the 1990s to a small residual net loss,
    with gains in temperate regions offsetting losses in the tropics. The global number hides the
    story: primary tropical forest keeps falling while plantations and regrowth rise.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="global forest area, 1990–2020 (FAO FRA)" widget="plotter" config={faoCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve against its geography. The global total — about 4.06 billion hectares in
    2020, some 31% of land area — is a net of two opposing movements: temperate and boreal
    area <em>rising</em> (farmland abandonment, plantations, fire suppression) and tropical
    forest <em>falling</em>, driven overwhelmingly by agriculture — pasture for cattle in
    Amazonia, smallholder and plantation crops elsewhere. The netting is exactly why the
    headline number needs the primary-forest and degradation data alongside it: a hectare of
    new plantation does not replace a hectare of primary rainforest in carbon, biodiversity
    or water regulation, any more than a new suburb replaces a cathedral. FAO's own reporting
    distinguishes naturally regenerating from planted forest for precisely this reason.
  </p>

  <h2>What foresters actually do</h2>
  <p>
    The working cycle is: know the trees (identification and <em>silvics</em> — each species'
    tolerance of shade, fire, frost and competition, the knowledge that decides what can
    regenerate where), measure the forest (<em>mensuration</em>: plot-based inventory of
    species, diameter, height and volume; the increment core that reads age and growth from
    rings), decide the treatment (<em>silviculture</em>), plan across decades (management
    plans with rotations, allowable cuts and habitat objectives), and defend the result
    (<em>protection</em> from fire, pests and disease). Silvicultural systems are the craft's
    core vocabulary: <em>clearcutting</em> (all trees removed — suits shade-intolerant,
    even-aged species like pines, at maximum ecological cost), <em>shelterwood</em> (seed
    trees left through regeneration, then removed), <em>selection</em> (individual or small
    groups harvested, continuous uneven-aged cover — the near-natural system, demanding the
    most skill). Behind all of it
    stands ecology — because a managed forest is still an ecosystem, run on sunlight and soil.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three harder topics show where the discipline is heading. First,
    <strong>fire as management problem</strong>: a century of aggressive suppression in
    fire-adapted western US and Australian forests built fuel loads that now drive
    catastrophic fires — the policy correction (prescribed burning, thinning, letting
    low-intensity fire do its ecological work) is one of forestry's hardest re-learnings,
    because it means <em>using</em> the disturbance the profession was built to exclude.
    Second, <strong>the carbon arithmetic</strong>: wood in long-lived products stores
    carbon, but only decades of regrowth repay a harvest's initial carbon debt; this is why
    carbon accounting distinguishes storage in forest, storage in products and substitution
    effects — and why "wood is climate-neutral" is true only under specific, checkable
    assumptions (the 2010s debates over counting forest bioenergy as carbon-neutral made the
    subtlety famous). Third, <strong>ownership as destiny</strong>: who owns a forest predicts
    its management better than its ecology does — public forests, industrial private,
    smallholder and community forests respond to different incentives, and the global
    experiment (China's collective forest reforms, Latin America's Indigenous titling,
    which studies associate with lower deforestation) suggests community tenure is among the
    most effective conservation instruments known. The through-line for the whole unit:
    forestry's founding insight — that a harvest must respect a growth rate — has scaled from
    Carlowitz's mine timbers to planetary carbon, and every generation of foresters has had
    to renegotiate what, exactly, is being sustained, and for whom.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SAF accreditation standards; FAO Global Forest Resources Assessment 2020; Nyland, <em>Silviculture: Concepts and Applications</em>; Carlowitz, <em>Sylvicultura oeconomica</em> (1713); Pinchot, <em>Breaking New Ground</em> (1947).</p>
</TopicPage>
